messages:
kevin: Quick Q — are we enforcing Content-Type: application/json on /v1/respond? I noticed some older clients send text/plain with a JSON body. Will our schema validator reject them?

lina: Yes — we flipped on strict header checking in v1.12.3. By default the gateway returns 415 if Content-Type != application/json for JSON endpoints.

sam: Oof, that will break a bunch of mobile customers. Do we have a compatibility toggle? @lina

lina: There's a per-route config flag `accept_legacy_content_types` (false by default). Ops can enable it for specific customers. It tries to parse body as JSON even if header is wrong.

jamie: Suggestion: instead of silently accepting, return 200 but include `X-Warn-Client: content-type-mismatch` header so clients see the deprecation. Otherwise they never fix it. :thinking_face:

devops-bot: deploy: rolled out gateway v1.12.3 -> prod (2025-11-10T14:02:17Z) :rocket:

maya: Also note streaming endpoints (ndjson) commonly use `application/x-ndjson` or `application/ndjson`. We should ensure our streaming parser handles those and doesn't trigger the JSON-only rule.

kevin: Good point. For structured outputs we rely on content-type to pick the parser and to validate schema (e.g., ndjson vs json). How does the validator treat nullable vs absent fields when streaming?

lina: Validator rules: for JSON endpoints we validate against the JSON Schema we ship. Behavior summary: missing optional props -> ok; explicit `null` for properties typed as `string` -> validation error unless `type: [\"string\",\"null\"]` is allowed. For streaming ndjson, each line is validated as an independent JSON document.

sam: Can we add an automatic shim: if `accept_legacy_content_types` true, attempt to parse body even with wrong header, but still run the same schema checks and attach `X-Compatibility: parsed-with-legacy` header?

jamie: Implementation note for eng-runtime: quick pseudo-code:

```
// on request
contentType = req.headers['content-type'] || 'application/json'
if (!isSupported(contentType) && route.acceptLegacy) {
  try {
    parsed = JSON.parse(req.body)
    req.parsed_body = parsed
    res.setHeader('X-Compatibility', 'parsed-with-legacy')
  } catch (e) {
    return res.status(415).send({ error: 'Unsupported Content-Type and body not valid JSON' })
  }
} else if (!isSupported(contentType)) {
  return res.status(415).send({ error: 'Unsupported Content-Type' })
}
// then run schema validation on req.parsed_body
```

kevin: That looks reasonable. What about clients that send `text/plain; charset=utf-8` — should we treat `text/*` as legacy JSON if body parses?

lina: We can allow `text/*` only under the route flag. Don't enable it globally. Also add a metric `legacy_content_type_accepted{route}` so PMs can see how many rely on it.

maya: For streaming, add support for `application/x-ndjson` and fallbacks `text/plain` lines if a route is marked streaming-compat. And document it in the API breaking-changes page: https://docs.redwood.ai/guides/content-type-compat (draft)

sam: I'll open a tiny migration PR for the mobile team that sets the flag for their org and adds the `X-Warn-Client` header.

kevin: Awesome — can someone add a small test matrix in the schema tests? Cases: correct header; text/plain valid JSON body; text/plain invalid JSON; ndjson streaming with application/x-ndjson and text/plain lines.

jamie: I'll add tests in runtime/acceptance/content_type_compat_test.go. Keep them lightweight.

kevin: Thanks all. If we agree, i'll put an RFC in eng-platform for the config and metrics plan.

lina: SGTM :thumbsup:
